{"title":"2015 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 19","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Shoelace_Theorem)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Shoelace Theorem)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(easiest_solution)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (easiest solution)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Pick&#39;s_Theorem,_very_much_recommended_to_learn_this)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Pick's Theorem, very much recommended to learn this)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Heron&#39;s_Formula,_Not_Recommended)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Heron's Formula, Not Recommended)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(Simple_Deduction)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (Simple Deduction)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(HOW_TO_THINK_CRITICALLY!!!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (HOW TO THINK CRITICALLY!!!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A triangle with vertices as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/e/68e8c6017962feb4293fbab0bba51887ba571656.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=(1,3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/0/f704a5156f7020d34e532d60908b4ca2a03b521d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B=(5,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/1/041b48d927275b860db2480c54937ad5229cf91e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C=(4,4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> is plotted on a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/7/d3733016a52c44c32f8ead49f5815f1996947f5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\times5$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> grid. What fraction of the grid is covered by the triangle?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/3/1d3903da82cf00ba34eeffc477bd4a3f65339802.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }\\frac{1}{6} \\qquad \\textbf{(B) }\\frac{1}{5} \\qquad \\textbf{(C) }\\frac{1}{4} \\qquad \\textbf{(D) }\\frac{1}{3} \\qquad \\textbf{(E) }\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"371\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/2/e52b5a181480dd60bedad1f91e8e9246b9f0c4fa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((1,0)--(1,5),linewidth(.5)); draw((2,0)--(2,5),linewidth(.5)); draw((3,0)--(3,5),linewidth(.5)); draw((4,0)--(4,5),linewidth(.5)); draw((5,0)--(5,5),linewidth(.5)); draw((6,0)--(6,5),linewidth(.5)); draw((0,1)--(6,1),linewidth(.5)); draw((0,2)--(6,2),linewidth(.5)); draw((0,3)--(6,3),linewidth(.5)); draw((0,4)--(6,4),linewidth(.5)); draw((0,5)--(6,5),linewidth(.5));  draw((0,0)--(0,6),EndArrow); draw((0,0)--(7,0),EndArrow); draw((1,3)--(4,4)--(5,1)--cycle); label(&quot;$y$&quot;,(0,6),W); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(7,0),S); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,(1,3),dir(210)); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,(5,1),SE); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,(4,4),dir(100)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"218\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is equal to half the product of its base and height.  By the Pythagorean Theorem, we find its height is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/c/d7c102e6d465673a7871e64ea35e2c43eeabc419.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1^2+2^2}=\\sqrt{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"119\" height=\"19\" />, and its base is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/4/6f4c5aeca5c4636b8eec4391a024e89c4e304d17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2^2+4^2}=\\sqrt{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"128\" height=\"19\" />.  We multiply these and divide by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to find the area of the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/d/45d8abcd96938150b1cef6c8c01f8a3f5fba7623.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{5 \\cdot 20}}2=\\frac{\\sqrt{100}}2=\\frac{10}2=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"209\" height=\"41\" />.  Since the grid has an area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651ba418a810d971dbc8a326d49a71fe1541fae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, the fraction of the grid covered by the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d56888852146e06fa5e4c793a6a1e6fb4e3fbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 5{30}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }\\frac{1}{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"107\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Note angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/0728bebb477a2636300c56851b6a6f9e382c1b83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> is right; thus, the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/0/7e00a2f05f00193e4de7f227a26bb0484896369c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1^2+3^2} \\times \\sqrt{1^2+3^2}\\times \\dfrac{1}{2}=10 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"309\" height=\"37\" />; thus, the fraction of the total is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/5/a45039eae70b11203962debbfcb0ab0dceb4f6aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{5}{30}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~\\dfrac{1}{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"107\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Shoelace_Theorem.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Shoelace_Theorem)\">Solution 3 (Shoelace Theorem)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Shoelace Theorem)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>By the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Shoelace_Theorem\" title=\"Shoelace Theorem\">Shoelace Theorem</a>, the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce416bb2442dcb21951fb2686bbec47b19ca5284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC=|\\dfrac{1}{2}(15+4+4-1-20-12)|=|\\dfrac{1}{2}(-10)|=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"440\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>This means the fraction of the total area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/5/a45039eae70b11203962debbfcb0ab0dceb4f6aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{5}{30}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~\\dfrac{1}{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"107\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_4_.28easiest_solution.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(easiest_solution)\">Solution 4 (easiest solution)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (easiest solution)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The smallest rectangle that follows the grid lines and completely encloses <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> has an area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> splits the rectangle into four triangles. The area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/0/680f60c4ed0f12f8b1e2149362c255f32e5bd564.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12 - (\\frac{4 \\cdot 2}{2}+\\frac{3 \\cdot 1}{2}+\\frac{3 \\cdot 1}{2}) = 12 - (4 + \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}) = 12 - 7 = 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"470\" height=\"37\" />. That means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> takes up <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/2/a8221bcc64dfb6d33684e9f06af617597b4ebd13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{30} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~\\frac{1}{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"107\" height=\"49\" /> of the grid.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Pick.27s_Theorem.2C_very_much_recommended_to_learn_this.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Pick's_Theorem,_very_much_recommended_to_learn_this)\">Solution 5 (Pick's Theorem, very much recommended to learn this)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Pick&#39;s Theorem, very much recommended to learn this)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Using <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pick%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Pick&#39;s Theorem\">Pick's Theorem</a>, the area of the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/4/2147b98c3a1e3601918ff39dacd0c2dc31f6888e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 + \\dfrac{4}{2} - 1=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"108\" height=\"37\" />. Therefore, the triangle takes up <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/9/de92151ba6bbaf926e085a39c06c9cd779d201ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{5}{30}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(A)}~\\frac{1}{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"106\" height=\"48\" /> of the grid.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Heron.27s_Formula.2C_Not_Recommended.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Heron's_Formula,_Not_Recommended)\">Solution 6 (Heron's Formula, Not Recommended)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Heron&#39;s Formula, Not Recommended)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We can find the lengths of the sides by using the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pythagorean_Theorem\" title=\"Pythagorean Theorem\">Pythagorean Theorem</a>. Then, we apply <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Heron%27s_Formula\" title=\"Heron&#39;s Formula\">Heron's Formula</a> to find the area. \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/8/4f8d590036ae9f5fb94c0d00a2b1a520769b93bc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{10}+2\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{10}+2\\sqrt{5}}{2}-\\sqrt{10}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{10}+2\\sqrt{5}}{2}-\\sqrt{10}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{10}+2\\sqrt{5}}{2}-2\\sqrt{5}\\right)}.\\]\" width=\"902\" height=\"64\" />\nThis simplifies to\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/e/76e900895ac2f3f2fe0650f9cba2b578daea5e30.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{10}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{10}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5}\\right)}.\\]\" width=\"599\" height=\"43\" />\nAgain, we simplify to get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/9/7b998dc0cac656fb6c4c2f7711394cb48729bace.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{5}\\right)}.\\]\" width=\"322\" height=\"43\" />\nThe middle two terms inside the square root multiply to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the first and last terms inside the square root multiply to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e598eda058334b8f222b70d15c7e7672caab7e4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{10}^2-\\sqrt{5}^2=10-5=5.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"205\" height=\"22\" /> This means that the area of the triangle is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8cc2b2222446e1d3ab796ec72e3361e9b43b6b7f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{5\\cdot 5}=5.\\]\" width=\"82\" height=\"18\" />\nThe area of the grid is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/5/ec5d405d9afbf0a9da30ed9531089bc1c9d10c4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\cdot 5=30.$\" width=\"77\" height=\"12\" /> Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/b/d8bb8781f39a3fffca0549eea392c485452e3523.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{30}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }\\frac{1}{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"107\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_7_.28Simple_Deduction.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(Simple_Deduction)\">Solution 7 (Simple Deduction)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (Simple Deduction)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>First, count the number of 1x1 squares inside the main triangle, both fully enclosed and partially (you should count 10). Then, upon closer inspection, most of the squares that are not a single unit on the triangle can be created by connecting another shape. The only exception is one square that is a single unit and one that would need 2 shapes connected to it to make a single unit. On average, you need to connect 2 squares to make a unit. Knowing this, if there are 10 squares and you require 2 shapes to make a unit, 10 divided by 2 equals 5, which is the area.5 is 1/6 of 30(the total of the graph) and so the final answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/b/d8bb8781f39a3fffca0549eea392c485452e3523.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{30}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) }\\frac{1}{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"107\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p><p>-Themathnerd3.14\n</p><p>~Octopus72 (minor edits)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28HOW_TO_THINK_CRITICALLY.21.21.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(HOW_TO_THINK_CRITICALLY!!!)\">Video Solution (HOW TO THINK CRITICALLY!!!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (HOW TO THINK CRITICALLY!!!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ZOEG-KfBA2E\">https://youtu.be/ZOEG-KfBA2E</a>\n</p><p>~Education, the Study of Everything\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/EyDGtLc6xGE\">https://youtu.be/EyDGtLc6xGE</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/j3QSD5eDpzU?t=507\">https://youtu.be/j3QSD5eDpzU?t=507</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_19&am